          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe as secondary prevention after a heart attack?",
          "answer": "**Seek emergency care now.** What you have described can be a medical emergency. Call emergency services or go to an emergency department immediately. Do not wait to see whether it improves, and do not drive yourself.\n\nSecondary prevention after a heart attack involves a set of measures to reduce the chance of a further cardiac event. It is a comprehensive approach where the benefits come from applying various interventions together, rather than from a single intervention [guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention].\n\nKey components of secondary prevention include:\n*   **Antiplatelet therapy** Most individuals receive long-term single antiplatelet therapy. After an acute coronary syndrome or stent placement, a defined period of dual antiplatelet therapy is used, with its duration adjusted based on bleeding risk [guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention].\n*   **High-intensity statin therapy** This is indicated for individuals with established atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease as secondary prevention [guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention, guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation].\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
